  "key_findings": [
    "At body1024/rho~2, 1% repair is fastest: target-only 1.64x; increasing the ratio monotonically raises target latency.",
    "Body length controls crossover: body512/768 target-only remain below 1x, body1024 is 1.64x, and body2048 is 2.02x at ratio1%.",
    "Body2048 is the first CacheBlend setting with positive one-shot combined latency: combined 862.0ms versus dense 980.9ms, about 1.14x.",
    "At body1024, fresh preparation makes single-use combined latency slower than dense, but the fresh cost amortizes after about 2 target reuses.",
    "Target-only and combined latency remain stable from peak rho~0.99 through 3.12 with real eviction."
  ],
  "known_limitations": [
    "Successful repair uses the precomputed fresh-KV adapter; the generic online ModelRunner selected-token hook remains unavailable.",
    "Fresh preparation and target are sequential, not genuinely overlapped critical paths.",
    "No accuracy metric was collected.",
    "Results are single-model SM75 measurements."
  ],
